Summary Dry seeds of Nigella sativa L. (2n=12) belongs to the family Ranunculaceae (rooted plant species
possessing cytological novelty and economic importance) are exposed to 0.01 mg L­1 concentration (permissible
dose laid down by WHO) of arsenic trioxide (As2O3; inorganic arsenic form available mostly in soil) for 12, 24,
48 and 96 h durations. The treatments are performed following the impregnation of ﬁlter papers with the test solution. Results suggest that in relation to control, As2O3 treatment at different durations can progressively reduce
germination rate, seedling growth, and divisional cell frequency and enhance cytological aberrations in mitotic
cells. Cytotoxicity induced by As2O3 in the studied plant species is clastogenic in nature (chromosome fragmentations, rings, bridges, and micronuclei) as well as it is found to affect spindle formation and function (polyploid
cell formation and tripolar organization of chromosomes) and cellular metabolism (formation of giant cells of
variable shapes). Polyploid (2n>12) and giant cells are frequent aberration types recorded across the duration of
treatments. The investigation is signiﬁcant for the screening of arsenic-tolerant genotypes in relation to consumable dietary plant product(s).
Key words Nigella sativa, Arsenic trioxide, Permissible dose, Cytotoxicity, Growth inhibition.

Arsenic (As), toxic metalloid, is one of the major
environmental pollutants contaminating water and soil
(Cullen and Reimer 1989, Smedley and Kinniburgh
2002, Finnegan and Chen 2012) through industrial applications (mining, coal combustion, agricultural practices, semiconductors, etc.) and natural process. Arsenic
contaminated groundwater is a serious threat to human
health and livestock in different districts of West Bengal,
India (Mondal et al. 2010), and as a whole on Indian
sub-continent (Nordstrom 2002). Furthermore, arsenic
toxicity is worldwide concern regarding human health
hazards (Abdul et al. 2015), as it gets integrated into human food chain speciﬁcally through rice crop contamination (Meharg et al. 2009, Finnegan and Chen 2012).
Therefore, rooted plants (possessing edible products),
principal producer of ecosystem and primary site of biointeraction with soil deposited arsenic compounds, are
prime components of toxicity assessment and knowledge
of which can be explored for human health beneﬁts.
* Corresponding author, e-mail: dattaanimesh@gmail.com
DOI: 10.1508/cytologia.84.215

The present investigation describes the effect of
arsenic trioxide (As2O3) on seed germination and seedling growth, dividing cell frequency and chromosomal
architecture at 0.01 mg L­1 concentration (permissible
dose limit of arsenic in humans laid down by WHO̶
Chakraborti et al. 2009) for variable (12 to 96 h) durations of treatment using Nigella sativa L. (Family: Ranunculaceae, black cumin) as test system. The studied
plant species (2n=12) is selected because of its cytological novelty (Saha and Datta 2002) and it yields spice of
commerce apart from possessing immense therapeutic
signiﬁcance (Datta et al. 2012). As2O3 used as a source
of elementary arsenic is prevalent in an aquatic ecosystem and is reported to induce mutation and DNA damage in animals (Patlolla and Tchounwou 2005, Soriano
et al. 2008) and humans (Stevens et al. 2010). On the
contrary, As2O3 is also used as a drug against different
diseases in China and Greece (Swindell et al. 2013) as
well as used in the treatment of acute promyelocytic leukemia (Sanz et al. 2009).

