Materials and methods
Germplasm and treatments
Seed stock (6.34% moisture content; seed size
̶2.63 0.28 mm 1.57 0.04 mm; 100 seed weight
̶0.26 g) of Nigella sativa L. (Ranunculaceae) was
obtained as breeders seeds from medicinal plant garden,
Narendrapur Ramkrishna Mission, West Bengal, India.
In each duration of treatment (12, 24, 48 and 96 h),
100 dry ﬁlled seeds of N. sativa were placed into Petri
plates containing Whatman No. 1 ﬁlter papers impregnated with 0.01 mg L­1 (permissible dose laid down by
WHO; test solution was prepared using deionized water)
As2O3 (Merck AR grade) continuously to keep them
moist. A control set (100 seeds) was maintained using
deionized H2O. Such treatment mode was performed
to provide near environmental niche. All treatments
were performed under similar laboratory conditions at
21 1 C.
Assessment of seed germination and seedling growth
Seed germination rate and seedling growth were
studied on the 13th day days from treatment. Random
25 seedlings from each treatment including control were
measured in a millimeter graph paper. Lethality (from
germination rate) and injury (seedling growth) caused
due to treatments were calculated in relation to percent
to control as was suggested by Konzak et al. (1965).
Mitotic study
Six healthy roots of about 2 mm from each treatment
(including control) were cut, ﬁxed in 1 : 3 acetic ethanol
for overnight and preserved in 70% ethanol under refrigeration. Roots were stained in aceto-orcein–1 M HCl
(9 : 1) and the root tips were squashed in 45% acetic acid
on glass slides. For each treatment, 3 replica slides were
prepared and observed under a Leitz Laborlux S compound microscope and suitable cells were photographed
using E3 Scientiﬁc Camera attached to the microscope.
Mitotic index (number of dividing cells/total cells scored
100) and, aberration types and their frequencies were
studied.

Cytologia 84(3)

Statistical analyses
Signiﬁcant variations between/among the treatments
(including control) were assessed for attributes like
seedling growth, mitotic index and total mitotic aberration frequencies in dividing cells using one way analysis
of variance (ANOVA) and following computation of
critical difference (CD) at 0.05 probability level.
Results and discussion
Seed germination and seedling growth
Seed germination and seedling length are studied as
immediate biological effects induced by As2O3 at different durations and the data procured are documented
in Table 1. Compared to control (82%), germination rate
reduces in treatments (73 to 55%) and the reduction is
duration-dependent. Lethality ascertained from germination rate and calculated as a percent to control is found
to range from 10.98 to 32.93%, thereby suggesting that
the employed durations of As2O3 at permissible concentration are sub-lethal in nature and LD50 could not be
reached. Compared to control (27.78 0.08 mm), seedling
length reduces (16.20 1.02 to 11.17 0.89 mm) signiﬁcantly (p<0.05) in treatments and the reduction is also
duration-dependent. Results indicate that As2O3 treatments can inhibit germination rate and seedling growth
in the studied species, which can be equated to genetic
loss arising as the consequences of chromosomal aberrations in mitotic cells. Growth inhibition caused due
to heavy metals is also reported in Helianthus annuus
(Chakravarty and Srivastava 1992), Lens culinaris (Kiran and Șahin 2006), Capsicum annuum (Aslam et al.
2014), N. sativa (Kumbhakar et al. 2018), and Allium
cepa (Gupta et al. 2018).
Mitotic consequences
Mitotic index, types, and frequency of mitotic anomalies studied in test materials are presented in Table 2.
In relation to control, duration-dependent signiﬁcant
(p<0.05) reduction in dividing cell frequency is studied
following As2O3 treatments. The mitodepressive effect
due to treating agent may be the outcome of the slower
progression of the cell cycle from S phase to M phase
(Patlolla et al. 2012) or blockage at G2 phase of the cell

